---
title: How to assign multiple agents to a ticket
sidebar:
  order: 6
---
A support ticket can be assigned to more than one agent at the same time, so several teammates can collaborate on a single ticket without one of them having to hand it off. Here's how assignment works and how to manage it.

#### **Steps to Assign Agents to a Ticket:**

1. **Open the Ticket:**
   - Navigate to **Tickets** in the sidebar menu and click on the ticket you want to assign.
1. **Open the Assign Agent Control:**
   - In the ticket details page, click the **Assign Agent** button (it shows the current agents' avatars and names once a ticket has assignees).
1. **Search and Select Agents:**
   - A search box appears — type a name to filter team members.
   - Click an agent to add them to the ticket. Click an already-selected agent again to remove them.
   - You can select as many agents as the ticket needs; there's no fixed limit.
1. **Changes Save Immediately:**
   - Each time you add or remove an agent, the assignment is saved right away — there's no separate save step.

#### **How Assignment Is Displayed:**

- The **Assign Agent** button shows the first assigned agent's avatar and name, plus a `+N` count when more than one agent is assigned (e.g., "Jordan +2").
- Up to three agent avatars are shown stacked on the button; the name order has no special meaning, it's just the order agents were returned in.

#### **The "Also on This Ticket" Banner:**

- When you open a ticket that has other agents assigned besides yourself, a small banner appears just above the reply box (e.g., *"Priya is also on this ticket"* or *"Priya, Jordan are also on this ticket"*).
- This is a quick way to know who else is already working the ticket before you reply, so you don't duplicate a response.

#### **Who Can Assign Agents:**

- Only workspace **Admins** and **Managers** can open the Assign Agent control and change assignments.
- Regular team members can still see who's assigned to a ticket, but the assignment control isn't shown to them.

By assigning multiple agents when a ticket needs more than one person's attention (e.g., a technical issue that also needs a billing specialist), you keep everyone involved aware of the ticket in one place instead of splitting the conversation.
